meta { title = "Byte streams", description = "Copy binary data with a reusable ByteBuffer and generic readers and writers.", order = 8, group = "Library reference" }

Use ByteReader and ByteWriter from std/stream when a function needs to move bytes between endpoints. A File implements both traits. A BufferedReader implements ByteReader and a BufferedWriter implements ByteWriter.

h2 { "Copy a file with one reusable buffer" }

Place a file named source.bin in the working directory. This program writes two copies. The same pump works with raw files and with buffered files. finish flushes the buffered output and reports any final write error.

codeblock { language = kalandra, """from "std/stream" import ByteReader, ByteWriter, StreamError, readInto, writeAll
from "std/fs" import open, close, OpenMode, FsError, bufferedReader, bufferedWriter, finish
from "std/buffer" import ByteBuffer, byteBuffer, BufferError

fn pump[R: ByteReader, W: ByteWriter](source: R, target: W, mut chunk: ByteBuffer) -> u64 ! StreamError:
  total: u64 = 0
  while true:
    count = try readInto(source, chunk, 65536)
    if count == 0:
      break
    try writeAll(target, chunk)
    total = total + count
  return total

fn main() -> unit ! (FsError | StreamError | BufferError):
  chunk = try byteBuffer(65536)
  source = try open("source.bin", OpenMode.Read)
  target = try open("target.bin", OpenMode.Create)
  total = try pump(source, target, chunk)
  try close(target)
  try close(source)
  reader = try bufferedReader(try open("target.bin", OpenMode.Read), 8192)
  writer = try bufferedWriter(try open("buffered.bin", OpenMode.Create), 4096)
  again = try pump(reader, writer, chunk)
  try finish(writer)
  try close(reader)
  return ()
""" }

h2 { "Read into a buffer" }

readInto(reader, buffer, maxBytes) replaces the initialized contents of buffer. readAppend(reader, buffer, maxBytes) extends them. Both return the number of newly read bytes as u64. A positive request returning zero means end of input. A short positive read is data to process, and a zero-byte request cannot tell you whether more input is available.

h2 { "Write initialized bytes" }

write(writer, buffer) returns the number of bytes accepted in one write. writeAll(writer, buffer) returns unit once it has written the entire initialized prefix. A write may already have delivered some bytes when it reports an error. Handle StreamError at the boundary that decides whether to retry or report the failure.
